<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP, permitted for one feature and constrained in code rather than here.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    Ollama runs on the user's own computer and speaks plain http:// on port 11434. Since
    Android 9 the platform blocks cleartext by default, so without this the Ollama provider
    cannot connect at all.

    WHY THE RULE IS NOT WRITTEN HERE
    The rule this app actually wants is "cleartext to private addresses only". A network
    security config matches DNS names and IP literals; it CANNOT express a CIDR range, and the
    user's PC address is unknown at build time anyway. So the permission granted here is broad,
    and the real constraint lives in `LocalEndpoint` in :core-domain — the only code that can
    produce an Ollama base URL, which refuses anything that is not loopback, RFC 1918, RFC 3927,
    RFC 4193 or the Tailscale CGNAT range, and which is unit-tested.

    That is a stronger guarantee than this file could have made, not a weaker one.

    WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
    It does not weaken the other four providers. Their base URLs are https:// string constants
    compiled into the APK; permitting the app to speak cleartext is not an instruction to
    downgrade a request that already names https.

    DO NOT set usesCleartextTraffic in the manifest instead. That flag is coarser, it is ignored
    when this file is present, and it leaves no place to write down why.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
